'''''Typha domingensis''''', known commonly as '''southern cattail'''<ref>{{PLANTS|id=TYDO|taxon=Typha domingensis|accessdate=12 December 2015}}</ref> or '''cumbungi''', is a [[Perennial plant|perennial]] [[herbaceous]] plant of the [[genus]] ''[[Typha]]''.

==Taxonomy==
It was described by [[Christiaan Hendrik Persoon]] in 1807.<ref name = "POWO" />

==Distribution and habitat==
It is found throughout sutropical and tropical regions worldwide.<ref name = "POWO" /> It is sometimes found as a subdominant associate in [[mangroves|mangrove ecosystems]] such as the [[Petenes mangroves]] ecoregion of [[Yucatán (state)|Yucatán]].<ref name=":0">World Wildlife Fund. Eds. M. McGinley, C. M. Hogan, & C. Cleveland. 2010. [http://www.eoearth.org/article/Petenes_mangroves?topic=49597 ''Petenes mangroves''.] Encyclopedia of Earth. National Council for Science and the Environment. Washington, DC.</ref> The Southern Cattail grows between 2.0 and 2.5 meters in length and has flat sheaths to protect its core. It thrives in [[marsh]]es and ecosystems where the land has a similarity to [[wetland]]s. It can also survive in [[Salinity|high salinity]] water sources, making it much more resilient than similar species to this kind of [[Typha|cattail]].

==Uses==
In the [[Mesopotamian Marshes]] of southern [[Iraq]], [[Khirret]] is a dessert made from the pollen of this plant.<ref>{{ cite web | title=Khirret | url=https://www.atlasobscura.com/foods/khirret-iraqi-marsh-pollen-candy}}</ref>{{Full citation needed|date=March 2026}}  In [[Turkey|Turkish]] [[folk medicine]] the female [[inflorescence]]s of this plant and other ''Typha'' are used externally to treat wounds such as burns. Extracts of ''T. domingensis'' have been demonstrated to have wound healing properties in rat models.<ref name=":1">Akkol, E. K., et al. (2011). [http://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0378874110008214 The potential role of female flowers inflorescence of ''Typha domingensis'' Pers. in wound management.] ''Journal of Ethnopharmacology'' 133(3) 1027-32.</ref>

Water extracts of the fruit, female flower and male flower of ''Typha domingensis'' exhibit iron chelating activity as well as [[superoxide]] and [[nitric oxide]] scavenging activities. By contrast, only the fruit and female flower extracts were found to have [[alpha-glucosidase]] inhibitory activity.<ref name=":2">Chai TT, Mohan M, Ong HC, Wong FC (2014) [http://www.tjpr.org/vol13_no1/2014_13_1_10.php Antioxidant, iron-chelating and anti-glucosidase activities of ''Typha domingensis'' Pers. (Typhaceae)]. Tropical Journal of Pharmaceutical Research 13(1): 67-72.</ref> A partially purified, proanthocyanidin-rich butanol fraction of the fruit was shown to be a competitive inhibitor of [[alpha-glucosidase]], which also had concurrent antioxidant activity.<ref>Chai TT, Chiam MJ, Lau CH, Mohd Ismail NI, Ong HC, Abd Manan F, Wong FC  (2015) [http://www.tjpr.org/home/abstract.php?abstractTitle=Alpha-glucosidase%20inhibitory%20and%20antioxidant%20activity%20of%20solvent%20extracts%20and%20fractions%20of%20Typha%20domingensis%20(Typhaceae)%20fruit&id=286 Alpha-glucosidase inhibitory and antioxidant activity of solvent extracts and fractions of ''Typha domingensis'' (Typhaceae) fruit]. Tropical Journal of Pharmaceutical Research 14 (11): 1983-1990.</ref>

''Typha domingensis'' can reduce water contamination by enterobacteria, which are pathogens, by up to 98 percent.<ref name=":3">[https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2015/03/150313083400.htm Common weed revealed to diminish water pollution]</ref>{{Full citation needed|date=March 2026}} 

It improves the water quality and the Southern Cattail is used in [[Artisan|artisanal crafts]] due to their wide reed sheaths which ease the [[weaving]] process.<ref name=":3" /><ref>{{cite journal |last1=Maldonado |first1=Guadalupe |last2=Voeks |first2=Robert |title=The Paradox of Culturally Useful Invasive Species: Southern Cattail (Typha domingensis) Crafts of Lake Pátzcuaro, Mexico |journal=Journal of Latin American Geography |date=2021 |volume=20 |issue=3 |pages=148–174 |doi=10.1353/lag.0.0174}}</ref>

Areas where the cattail and bulrush is harvested in much larger methods, such as the coastal areas of [[San Jeronimo, Patzcuaro]] and [[Tzintzuntzan, Michoacán]] seem to exhibit larger than average cattail sizes, lengths, and population density. It's within these areas that at times, the amount of reproducing cattail can prove to be too much as it overruns some [[Agricultural land|farm land]], so its planned from the surrounding villagers to be routinely harvested and cut down to a reasonable size monthly or whenever the population rises to an overrunning size. This is especially common during the months of August and September, the rainiest months that the surrounding villages seem to experience during the last months of [[Summer]].<ref name=":0" />

